<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avalokiteśvara</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Avalokiteshvara, Lokeśvara, Chenrezig, Guanyin, Kannon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Avalokiteśvara, the Lord who looks down, is a bodhisattva of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> associated with great compassion, known in Tibetan as Chenrezig and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> as Guanyin, Kannon or Gwaneum. Chapter 25 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra</a> describes him as a bodhisattva who hears the cries of beings and helps all who call on his name, in 33 manifestations, some of them female. He is depicted in a vast number of forms across Buddhist cultures, and his mantra, Oṃ maṇi padme hūṃ, is the most popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. He is the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> figure who entered the worship of ordinary Buddhists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> countries, and many Tibetan Buddhists hold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a> to be his manifestations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/avalokitesvara.jpg" alt="Avalokiteśvara" width="330" height="524" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hyougushi · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khasarpana_Lokesvara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name combines ava, down, lokita, looked, and īśvara, lord, and so means the lord who gazed down at the world. The usual Chinese name Guanyin, perceiving sound, comes from the form Avalokitasvara, which is now understood to be the original and appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> fragments from the fifth century. The reading of the name as an īśvara shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> influence, but the mainstream of his devotees kept the Buddhist rejection of any creator god.</p><p>Scholars have not agreed on the origin of his veneration. Some suggest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. The scholar Shu Hikosaka proposes that Mount Potalaka, his home in the Gaṇḍavyūha Sūtra, is Mount Potigai on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> border. Around 400 CE the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> saw offerings made to him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> a female form, the White-Robed Avalokiteśvara, is worshipped as a divine mother. He is counted among the Four Great Bodhisattvas, with Mañjuśrī, Samantabhadra and Kṣitigarbha, and assists Amitabha Buddha. A popular story tells that he vowed to rest only when all beings were freed from saṃsāra. His head split into eleven pieces from the effort of grasping their needs, and Amitābha gave him eleven heads; his arms shattered as he reached out, and Amitābha gave him a thousand arms. This Thousand-armed form is among his most popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Tibetan tradition has the goddess Tara arise from one of his tears.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> the central mantra is Oṃ maṇi padme hūṃ. Reciting it on prayer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> is the most popular religious practice, and prayer wheels carry repetitions of it. Its link with him is first documented in the Kāraṇḍavyūhasūtra, dated to around the late 4th to early 5th century. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> the 82-syllable Nīlakaṇṭha Dhāraṇī, the Great Compassion Mantra, is very popular.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, where he is called Nātha, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> is almost exclusively <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a>, yet he is the one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> deity to enter the worship of ordinary Buddhists. Many there believe Nātha to be the Buddha yet to come, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya</a>, though the iconography, with an image of Amitābha on the crown, points to Avalokiteśvara. In Myanmar he is Lokanat and in Thailand Lokesvara.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Avalokiteśvara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
